course: dictionary
lesson: 10002
title: Two meanings of one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Television is a noun with two main meanings. First, a television is a machine with a screen that shows programmes with pictures and sound. Second, television is the whole system of sending programmes, and the programmes themselves.
explain: Look at this sentence: «Furniture and televisions that tip over injure many young children every year.» Here televisions means the machines, because a machine can tip over and a system of programmes cannot. Ask yourself: which meaning fits the sentence?
explain: Now look at this one: «A broadcast, such as radio or television, sends the same message from one sender to many people at once.» Here television means the system of sending programmes, not a machine. Short forms help too: TV is a short way of saying television.
fact: A television is a machine with a screen; television can also mean the system of sending programmes.
fact: «Televisions» in the plural means more than one machine.
fact: When television means the system or the programmes, it is uncountable: you say watch television, not watch a television.
fact: A broadcast, such as radio or television, sends the same message from one sender to many people at once.
fact: Television came in the 1920s, long after Queen Victoria died in 1901.
exercise: «Furniture and televisions that tip over injure many young children every year.» What does televisions mean here? | Pictures painted on a wall | The system of sending programmes | *Machines with screens | Radios that play music
exercise: «A broadcast, such as radio or television, sends the same message from one sender to many people at once.» What does television mean here? | The machine with a screen | A kind of telephone | *The system of sending programmes | A type of furniture
exercise: What does TV stand for? | Track Viewer | *Television | Talking Visitor | Tiny Vehicle
test: «US television shows have their own labels, such as TV-Y for young children, TV-PG and TV-14.» What are these labels for? | How loud the sound is | *Telling viewers which age a show suits | How many people made the show | The colour of the screen
test: «In 1957 a BBC television programme showed Swiss farmers picking spaghetti from trees, and many viewers believed it.» What is a programme here? | *A show sent out on television | A machine that shows pictures | A kind of tree | A message sent by radio only
exam: In «Furniture and televisions that tip over injure many young children every year», what does televisions mean? | Walls that show pictures | Furniture that has a screen | The system of sending programmes | *Machines with screens that show programmes
